How does Eureka determine service availability? (Short)

Heartbeat (Lease Renewal): Each service sends a heartbeat to Eureka every 30 seconds (default).
Lease Expiration: If Eureka doesn't receive a heartbeat for 90 seconds (default), it marks the service as DOWN or removes it (if self-preservation is disabled).

Default Configuration:

eureka:
  instance: in each services - properties
    lease-renewal-interval-in-seconds: 30
    lease-expiration-duration-in-seconds: 90


Self-Preservation Mode in Eureka (Short)

A safety feature that prevents Eureka from removing services during temporary network issues.
If many heartbeats are missed, Eureka assumes a network problem instead of service failures.
This helps avoid mass deregistration of healthy services.

Default Configuration:

eureka:
  server:
    enable-self-preservation: true
    renewal-percent-threshold: 0.85
    eviction-interval-timer-in-ms: 60000


What is a Lease in Eureka? (Short)

A lease is an agreement between a service and the Eureka server that determines how long the service is considered alive.
The service renews the lease by sending a heartbeat every 30 seconds (default).
If no heartbeat is received within 90 seconds (default), Eureka removes the service from the registry.

Default Configuration:

eureka:
  instance:
    lease-renewal-interval-in-seconds: 30
    lease-expiration-duration-in-seconds: 90


